select 'sociological-theory', 'Conflict theories: Modern approaches', 'C. Wright Mills has been called the founder of modern conflict theory. In Mills''s view, social structures are created through conflict between people with differing interests and resources. Individuals and resources, in turn, are influenced by these structures and by the "unequal distribution of power and resources in the society." The power elite of American society, (i.e., the military–industrial complex) had "emerged from the fusion of the corporate elite, the Pentagon, and the executive branch of government." Mills argued that the interests of this elite were opposed to those of the people. He theorized that the policies of the power elite would result in "increased escalation of conflict, production of weapons of mass destruction, and possibly the annihilation of the human race."', 'Wikipedia: Conflict theories', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Conflict_theories&oldid=1375940212', 'CC BY-SA 4.0. Wikipedia contributors (see page history). Excerpted and split into passages.'
where not exists (select 1 from public.content_chunks where topic_id = 'sociological-theory' and body = 'C. Wright Mills has been called the founder of modern conflict theory. In Mills''s view, social structures are created through conflict between people with differing interests and resources. Individuals and resources, in turn, are influenced by these structures and by the "unequal distribution of power and resources in the society." The power elite of American society, (i.e., the military–industrial complex) had "emerged from the fusion of the corporate elite, the Pentagon, and the executive branch of government." Mills argued that the interests of this elite were opposed to those of the people. He theorized that the policies of the power elite would result in "increased escalation of conflict, production of weapons of mass destruction, and possibly the annihilation of the human race."');
